## Why This Is Asked

Assess understanding of low-level synchronization, atomic operations, and memory ordering in a practical, portable way.

## Key Concepts

- Spinlock using atomic_flag
- Memory ordering: acquire on lock, release on unlock
- Exponential backoff with PAUSE to reduce contention
- Correctness test with concurrent increments

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <immintrin.h>

typedef struct { atomic_flag flag; } spinlock;

static inline void spinlock_init(spinlock* s) {
  atomic_flag_clear(&s->flag);
}

static inline void spin_lock(spinlock* s) {
  while (atomic_flag_test_and_set_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_acquire)) {
    _mm_pause();
    // small exponential backoff
    for (int b = 1; b < 1024; b <<= 1) {
      _mm_pause();
    }
  }
}

static inline void spin_unlock(spinlock* s) {
  atomic_flag_clear_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_release);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for NUMA systems to reduce cross-socket traffic?
- What are the trade-offs of using a ticket-lock vs this spinlock in high-contention workloads?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests low-level synchronization primitives, memory ordering, and ABA avoidance in a tiny mailbox primitive.

## Key Concepts
- Atomic CAS on a 64-bit slot; sequence counters prevent ABA.
- Memory_order_release on publish, memory_order_acquire on receive for visibility.
- Fill-empty state via payload==0 heuristic for simplicity; robust designs can reserve a dedicated marker.

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { _Atomic uint64_t slot; } Mailbox;
void mailbox_init(Mailbox *m){ atomic_init(&m->slot, 0); }
bool mailbox_send(Mailbox *m, uint32_t payload){
  uint64_t cur = atomic_load_explicit(&m->slot, memory_order_relaxed);
  uint32_t seq = (uint32_t)(cur>>32);
  uint64_t want = ((uint64_t)seq<<32);
  uint64_t next = (((uint64_t)(seq+1))<<32) | payload;
  return atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&m->slot, &want, next);
}
bool mailbox_recv(Mailbox *m, uint32_t *out){
  uint64_t cur = atomic_load_explicit(&m->slot, memory_order_acquire);
  uint32_t seq = (uint32_t)(cur>>32);
  uint32_t payload = (uint32_t)(cur & 0xFFFFFFFF);
  if (payload == 0) return false;
  uint64_t next = (((uint64_t)(seq+1))<<32) | 0u;
  if (atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&m->slot, &cur, next)){
    *out = payload; return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multiple producers while keeping SPSC guarantees?
- What microarchitectural pitfalls exist (false sharing, cache lines) and how would you mitigate them?

## Why This Is Asked
The barrier tests fundamental atomic sequencing and memory-order choices without OS primitives.

## Key Concepts
- Barrier reuse with generation counter
- Atomic increments and acquire/release semantics
- False sharing avoidance via padding
- Stress testing for thread skew and latency

## Code Example
