## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of basic memory management without dynamic allocation, a common pattern in low-latency systems.

## Key Concepts

- Fixed-size pool basics
- Free-list within block memory
- Alignment and safety checks
- Simple double-free detection (debug)

## Code Example

```c
#define BLOCK_SIZE 64
#define N_BLOCKS 1024
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; unsigned char payload[BLOCK_SIZE - sizeof(struct Block*)]; } Block;
static Block blocks[N_BLOCKS];
static Block* free_list;

void pool_init(void){
  for(int i=0;i<N_BLOCKS-1;i++) blocks[i].next = &blocks[i+1];
  blocks[N_BLOCKS-1].next = NULL;
  free_list = &blocks[0];
}
void* pool_alloc(void){
  if(!free_list) return NULL;
  Block* b = free_list;
  free_list = b->next;
  return (void*)b->payload;
}
void pool_free(void* p){
  Block* b = (Block*)((char*)p - offsetof(Block, payload));
  b->next = free_list;
  free_list = b;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect double-free in a release build?
- How would you extend this to multi-threaded use without locks?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of per-thread buffering, cache-line alignment, and atomic merges to minimize contention in high-frequency workloads.

## Key Concepts
- Thread-local buffers to avoid contention
- Cache-line alignment to prevent false sharing
- Atomic merges with memory_order_relaxed for performance

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdalign.h>
#define NBINS 256

typedef struct {
  alignas(64) unsigned long long bins[NBINS];
} thread_hist_t;

unsigned long long global_bins[NBINS];

void add_sample(thread_hist_t* t, unsigned int v) {
  t->bins[v]++;
}

void merge_all_to_global(thread_hist_t* t) {
  for (int i = 0; i < NBINS; ++i)
    __atomic_fetch_add(&global_bins[i], t->bins[i], __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
  // optionally clear t->bins here
}
```

